  "description": "T1 - Tree of Heaven – Multi-stemmed, approximately 12m in height with a 6m lateral spread.\r\n\r\nProposed Works: Fell to ground level.\r\n\r\nReasoning:\r\n\r\n1. Safety concerns\r\nThis tree poses a genuine safety issue. There’s significant decay within the main limbs and an included union around 2.5 m. One of the larger limbs extends directly over a busy public footpath, and there’s a high risk of failure. Removing that limb would leave a large wound, leave the tree unbalanced, and severely reduce its already poor amenity value - effectively making it unsustainable to retain in its current form.\r\n\r\n2. Invasive species\r\nTree of Heaven is a recognised invasive non-native species in the UK (listed under Schedule 9 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981). It spreads quickly through root suckers and seed, outcompeting native species and reducing biodiversity. Giving this species TPO protection goes against current ecological guidance and local biodiversity objectives.\r\n\r\n3. Limited amenity value\r\nThis particular specimen is small, decayed, and poorly formed. It offers very limited visual or environmental benefit. Based on its condition, I struggle to see how it achieved a TEMPO score high enough to justify a TPO, and I’d appreciate a copy of the completed assessment form for review.",
